{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793873049825,"sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","part":{"partID":"prt_1ab2a502d001fD0EfqJSkkQTYV","sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","messageID":"msg_1ab2a4278001ZOmmqN5hI62wQK","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b650862051a1fc35","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && git diff 10474a9567 -- .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/template-contract.md .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md | head -220"},"output":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md\nindex f7738ba3c8..7b2a2d351c 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md\n@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ Records where the evilcharts reference came from, so what was measured and what\n **Source:** the evilcharts component library, `src/app/globals.css`, its `:root` and `.dark`\n custom-property blocks. Both blocks are carried verbatim beside this file as `source-globals.css`,\n so every converted hex can be checked against the `oklch()` it came from without reaching for the\n-library clone, which lives in another packet's research context.\n+library itself.\n **Read:** 2026-09-09\n \n ---\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md\nindex d9029e67ae..f7f18b9c2f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md\n@@ -146,11 +146,10 @@ Every name in the left column already routes to this packet, so a request carryi\n \n One of those rows is a substitution rather than a match, and the difference is worth saying plainly. **This corpus draws no arc-based pie or donut.** It does now draw a binned histogram, which is why `histogram` has moved out of this table and into the index above. Each of the two rows names what arrives instead and why it answers the question the reader asked. The other five rows are the same chart wearing a name the index happens not to use.\n \n-The same refusal holds for radar. Phase 13's final synthesis keeps `parallel-axes` as the\n-multi-dimensional answer and keeps both radar and arc-based pie out: mixed units do not become\n-honest by being normalised onto a polygon, and a reader can count the marks in `unit-grid` or\n-`unit-ring` instead of estimating an angle. That is a kept decision, not an unfinished gap.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:123-132]\n+The same refusal holds for radar. `parallel-axes` is the multi-dimensional answer, and both\n+radar and arc-based pie stay out: mixed units do not become honest by being normalised onto a\n+polygon, and a reader can count the marks in `unit-grid` or `unit-ring` instead of estimating an\n+angle. That is a kept decision, not an unfinished gap.\n \n A name that reaches neither this table nor a row in the index is still a gap to report. Sending a reader to a chart that answers a different question costs more than telling them the corpus has no form for it.\n \n@@ -173,8 +172,8 @@ renders.\n | Form | Why it is absent |\n | --- | --- |\n | sankey | The contract excludes forms that need a layout engine, and a flow diagram is one. A hand-drawn approximation of a sankey is less honest than saying the corpus does not draw one, because the thing a reader trusts in a flow diagram is that the ribbon widths were solved rather than eyeballed. If flow is ever genuinely wanted, the exclusion in the contract is what to revisit, not this row |\n-| radar | `parallel-axes` answers the same question with one scale per axis. A radar normalises every dimension onto one radial scale, which is honest when the dimensions share a unit and misleading when they do not, and the request that reaches this corpus almost always mixes units. The area a radar encloses also reads as a quantity and is not one, since it changes when the axes are reordered. The name itself routes to `sk-design-diagram`, which does have a radar file; this row is what to reach for once the request is here and is about values rather than structure, and `SKILL.md` carries the routing half. This phase keeps the omission after the shadcn comparison. [SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:123-132] |\n-| pie | The corpus draws no arc-based pie. A part-to-whole question is routed to `unit-grid` or `unit-ring`, where a reader counts filled marks or whole-number ticks instead of estimating slice angles. Phase 13 keeps that substitution after comparing the shadcn inventory. [SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:123-132] |\n+| radar | `parallel-axes` answers the same question with one scale per axis. A radar normalises every dimension onto one radial scale, which is honest when the dimensions share a unit and misleading when they do not, and the request that reaches this corpus almost always mixes units. The area a radar encloses also reads as a quantity and is not one, since it changes when the axes are reordered. The name itself routes to `sk-design-diagram`, which does have a radar file; this row is what to reach for once the request is here and is about values rather than structure, and `SKILL.md` carries the routing half. |\n+| pie | The corpus draws no arc-based pie. A part-to-whole question is routed to `unit-grid` or `unit-ring`, where a reader counts filled marks or whole-number ticks instead of estimating slice angles. |\n \n ---\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\nindex 54c4bd0c96..d9dfab073f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\n@@ -81,21 +81,17 @@ so the mapping is written here rather than inferred from a message.\n palette source and the rest of its own row. Lightness is colour, and ranking by it is what\n `importance` names.\n \n-### What the shadcn comparison kept\n-\n-The shadcn comparison measured a much tighter categorical light-theme set than the standalone\n-one: shadcn light has a 16.0° minimum adjacent hue gap and 1.72:1 minimum own/opposite-ground\n-contrast, while standalone categorical light has a 92.9° gap and 3.37:1 minimum own/opposite-ground\n-contrast. The dark measurements were 71.1° and 2.92:1/2.15:1 for shadcn, against 96.5° and\n-3.38:1/1.72:1 for standalone categorical. The numbers support keeping the standalone role split\n-and its existing gates rather than replacing it with a raw five-token ramp; they do not create a\n-new CVD or hue threshold here.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:55-66]\n-\n-The transferable part is semantic indirection: a named series owns a palette token through its\n-key, and a mark reaches that token through the class the key declares. That keeps the palette\n-roles here while making a per-key colour change one auditable edit in the chart's series block.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:123-132]\n+### Why the role split stays\n+\n+Keep the standalone role split and its existing gates rather than a raw five-token ramp. The\n+standalone categorical light set holds a 92.9° minimum adjacent hue gap and 3.37:1 minimum\n+own/opposite-ground contrast. The dark set holds 96.5° and 3.38:1/1.72:1. A tighter five-token\n+ramp gives up that separation. These figures describe the current set and do not create a new CVD\n+or hue threshold.\n+\n+A named series owns a palette token through its key, and a mark reaches that token through the\n+class the key declares. That keeps the palette roles here while making a per-key colour change one\n+auditable edit in the chart's series block.\n \n ### How to settle a row against this table\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/template-contract.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/template-contract.md\nindex 5310d5765e..bd307c7c5b 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/template-contract.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/template-contract.md\n@@ -751,11 +751,10 @@ templates.\n \n A card takes every colour from the palette custom properties and never from a literal. `--chart-surface` for its fill, `--chart-rule` for its border, `--chart-ink` for a name and a value, `--chart-muted` for a label. Each template redefines those under `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, so a card built this way inverts with the page and needs no theme code of its own. A literal colour looks right in whichever theme its author was using and disappears in the other, and only the theme nobody checks breaks.\n \n-This `stacked-area` row overrides an earlier figure from the research (`all four band values plus\n-the total`), which assumed per-x hit targets that were never built. Build a one-row card: the\n-band's series name plus its period total, per the `Card name` and `Rows` cells above. Not a\n-five-row card, and not a name-only card either, since a per-x reading is not buildable without\n-machinery this packet excludes.\n+The `stacked-area` card is one row: the band's series name plus its period total, per the\n+`Card name` and `Rows` cells above. It is not a five-row card listing every band value plus the\n+total, because that needs per-x hit targets the template does not have. It is not a name-only card\n+either, since a per-x reading is not buildable without machinery this packet excludes.\n \n ### A key takes the shape of its mark\n \n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md\nindex f7738ba3c8..7b2a2d351c 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/assets/style-reference/evilcharts/origin.md\n@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ Records where the evilcharts reference came from, so what was measured and what\n **Source:** the evilcharts component library, `src/app/globals.css`, its `:root` and `.dark`\n custom-property blocks. Both blocks are carried verbatim beside this file as `source-globals.css`,\n so every converted hex can be checked against the `oklch()` it came from without reaching for the\n-library clone, which lives in another packet's research context.\n+library itself.\n **Read:** 2026-09-09\n \n ---\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md\nindex d9029e67ae..f7f18b9c2f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/catalog.md\n@@ -146,11 +146,10 @@ Every name in the left column already routes to this packet, so a request carryi\n \n One of those rows i