{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793873080995,"sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","part":{"partID":"prt_1ab2ae94c001uy7CRCc83kJXkP","sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","messageID":"msg_1ab2adf09001nrlJihutbUF88T","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-87755cd131492176","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && git diff 10474a9567 -- .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md | head -80; echo \"=== chart version ===\"; grep -n \"^version:\" .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md"},"output":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\nindex af18252092..c0850baea8 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n name: sk-design-chart\n description: \"Chart authoring for sk-design: turn a reader's comparison into one catalog form, copy its file and ship a standalone HTML chart.\"\n allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n-version: 0.23.6.0\n+version: 0.23.7.0\n metadata:\n   packetKind: workflow\n ---\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\nindex 54c4bd0c96..d9dfab073f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\n@@ -81,21 +81,17 @@ so the mapping is written here rather than inferred from a message.\n palette source and the rest of its own row. Lightness is colour, and ranking by it is what\n `importance` names.\n \n-### What the shadcn comparison kept\n-\n-The shadcn comparison measured a much tighter categorical light-theme set than the standalone\n-one: shadcn light has a 16.0° minimum adjacent hue gap and 1.72:1 minimum own/opposite-ground\n-contrast, while standalone categorical light has a 92.9° gap and 3.37:1 minimum own/opposite-ground\n-contrast. The dark measurements were 71.1° and 2.92:1/2.15:1 for shadcn, against 96.5° and\n-3.38:1/1.72:1 for standalone categorical. The numbers support keeping the standalone role split\n-and its existing gates rather than replacing it with a raw five-token ramp; they do not create a\n-new CVD or hue threshold here.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:55-66]\n-\n-The transferable part is semantic indirection: a named series owns a palette token through its\n-key, and a mark reaches that token through the class the key declares. That keeps the palette\n-roles here while making a per-key colour change one auditable edit in the chart's series block.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:123-132]\n+### Why the role split stays\n+\n+Keep the standalone role split and its existing gates rather than a raw five-token ramp. The\n+standalone categorical light set holds a 92.9° minimum adjacent hue gap and 3.37:1 minimum\n+own/opposite-ground contrast. The dark set holds 96.5° and 3.38:1/1.72:1. A tighter five-token\n+ramp gives up that separation. These figures describe the current set and do not create a new CVD\n+or hue threshold.\n+\n+A named series owns a palette token through its key, and a mark reaches that token through the\n+class the key declares. That keeps the palette roles here while making a per-key colour change one\n+auditable edit in the chart's series block.\n \n ### How to settle a row against this table\n \n=== chart version ===\n5:version: 0.23.7.0\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\nindex af18252092..c0850baea8 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n name: sk-design-chart\n description: \"Chart authoring for sk-design: turn a reader's comparison into one catalog form, copy its file and ship a standalone HTML chart.\"\n allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n-version: 0.23.6.0\n+version: 0.23.7.0\n metadata:\n   packetKind: workflow\n ---\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\nindex 54c4bd0c96..d9dfab073f 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md\n@@ -81,21 +81,17 @@ so the mapping is written here rather than inferred from a message.\n palette source and the rest of its own row. Lightness is colour, and ranking by it is what\n `importance` names.\n \n-### What the shadcn comparison kept\n-\n-The shadcn comparison measured a much tighter categorical light-theme set than the standalone\n-one: shadcn light has a 16.0° minimum adjacent hue gap and 1.72:1 minimum own/opposite-ground\n-contrast, while standalone categorical light has a 92.9° gap and 3.37:1 minimum own/opposite-ground\n-contrast. The dark measurements were 71.1° and 2.92:1/2.15:1 for shadcn, against 96.5° and\n-3.38:1/1.72:1 for standalone categorical. The numbers support keeping the standalone role split\n-and its existing gates rather than replacing it with a raw five-token ramp; they do not create a\n-new CVD or hue threshold here.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:55-66]\n-\n-The transferable part is semantic indirection: a named series owns a palette token through its\n-key, and a mark reaches that token through the class the key declares. That keeps the palette\n-roles here while making a per-key colour change one auditable edit in the chart's series block.\n-[SOURCE: specs/sk-design/018-sk-design-parent-v2/001-sk-create-chart/013-shadcn-reference-research/research/lineages/luna/research.md:123-132]\n+### Why the role split stays\n+\n+Keep the standalone role split and its existing gates rather than a raw five-token ramp. The\n+standalone categorical light set holds a 92.9° minimum adjacent hue gap and 3.37:1 minimum\n+own/opposite-ground contrast. The dark set holds 96.5° and 3.38:1/1.72:1. A tighter five-token\n+ramp gives up that separation. These figures describe the current set and do not create a new CVD\n+or hue threshold.\n+\n+A named series owns a palette token through its key, and a mark reaches that token through the\n+class the key declares. That keeps the palette roles here while making a per-key colour change one\n+auditable edit in the chart's series block.\n \n ### How to settle a row against this table\n \n=== chart version ===\n5:version: 0.23.7.0\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && git diff 10474a9567 -- .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/references/color-system.md | head -80; echo \\\"=== chart version ===\\\"; grep -n \\\"^version:\\\" .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-chart/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793873078604,"end":1793873080995}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793873081004,"sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","part":{"partID":"prt_1ab2ae841002Hy5gescnabkU0V","sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","messageID":"msg_1ab2adf09001nrlJihutbUF88T","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ab61bdea153db95","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && git diff 10474a9567 -- .skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md .skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md .skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md README.md | head -220"},"output":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md\nindex 7da8b2bdda..a3f6c799d1 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md\n@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@ name: mcp-figma\n description: \"Figma CLI orchestrator: drives figma-ds-cli for terminal Figma design work, with an optional Figma MCP via Code Mode.\"\n compatibility: Requires Figma Desktop (open with a file), Node.js >=18, and the silships figma-ds-cli on PATH. macOS is the supported baseline; the optional Figma MCP uses this project's Code Mode.\n allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, mcp__code_mode__call_tool_chain]\n-version: 1.2.1.0\n+version: 1.2.2.0\n user-invocable: true\n ---\n \n@@ -229,7 +229,7 @@ Scripts: `scripts/install.sh` (install + verify), `scripts/doctor.sh` (report-on\n \n Examples: [`examples/README.md`](examples/README.md) - safe worked walkthroughs mirroring the playbook scenarios (safe connect + daemon health, read-only inspect/export, optional MCP context pull). They live outside the `references/`/`assets/` discovery roots, so they are linked here rather than auto-loaded by the router.\n \n-Related skills: `sk-design-md-generator` (a measured Style Reference of extracted design tokens, applied whenever a read/export feeds a decision), `sk-code` (adapting exports into an app), `mcp-code-mode` (the optional MCP transport), `mcp-chrome-devtools` (browser preview only), and `system-spec-kit` when packet documentation or memory continuity applies. When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-design-fundamentals` and decide it there.\n+Related skills: `sk-design-md-generator` (a measured Style Reference of extracted design tokens, applied whenever a read/export feeds a decision), `sk-code` (adapting exports into an app), `mcp-code-mode` (the optional MCP transport), `mcp-chrome-devtools` (browser preview only), and `system-spec-kit` when packet documentation or memory continuity applies. When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) and decide it there.\n \n A Figma read that feeds code ends at the five-pass check in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md`.\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md\nindex 5ec937cf72..5d83d0e061 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md\n@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@ PRE: Requires INSPECT-001 PASS (or MCP-001 PASS for the Framelink path), Figma D\n \n ### Optional Supplemental Checks\n \n-Repeat Pass 5 contrast reads against the background each control actually sits on, not the page background, and compare them with the target `sk-design-fundamentals` sets.\n+Repeat Pass 5 contrast reads against the background each control actually sits on, not the page background, and compare them with the target `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) sets.\n \n ---\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md\nindex a83eefe756..45c3aeb45b 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md\n@@ -12,7 +12,7 @@ This document captures the realistic user-testing contract, current behavior, ex\n \n ## 1. OVERVIEW\n \n-This scenario validates token drift reporting for `F2C-002`. It focuses on confirming that a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift and handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`, never rounded silently into code.\n+This scenario validates token drift reporting for `F2C-002`. It focuses on confirming that a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift and handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`), never rounded silently into code.\n \n ### Why This Matters\n \n@@ -24,13 +24,13 @@ A design library can be newer than the token package an application installs, an\n \n Operators run the exact prompt and command sequence for `F2C-002` and confirm the expected signals without contradictory evidence.\n \n-- Objective: confirm a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift and handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`.\n+- Objective: confirm a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift and handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`).\n - Real user request: `This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.`\n - Prompt: `This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.`\n-- Expected execution process: read the node without changing it, record the authored frame width W, compare each value against the installed tokens first and the design scales second, report every unmatched value as drift with the nearest installed token and nearest scale step, then hand the decision to `sk-design-fundamentals` as the reference's workflow step requires.\n-- Expected signals: the `18px` padding and `44px` gap are both listed as drift with their nearest installed equivalent and nearest scale step. The reply names `sk-design-fundamentals` as the owner of the decision, and no rounded value appears in code. A pass that cannot run is marked `NOT RUN` with a reason and the Figma check verdict is `INCOMPLETE`.\n-- Desired user-visible outcome: the agent reports each drift value with its nearest installed equivalent and the five-pass evidence, and explains that the design decision is handed to `sk-design-fundamentals` rather than made in code.\n-- Pass/fail: PASS if every unmatched value is reported as drift, the nearest token and scale step are named, and the decision is handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`. The verdict is `PASS` when all passes pass and `INCOMPLETE` when any pass is `NOT RUN` with a reason. FAIL if a value is rounded or written into code without being reported, or if `PASS` is reported despite a `NOT RUN` pass.\n+- Expected execution process: read the node without changing it, record the authored frame width W, compare each value against the installed tokens first and the design scales second, report every unmatched value as drift with the nearest installed token and nearest scale step, then hand the decision to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) as the reference's workflow step requires.\n+- Expected signals: the `18px` padding and `44px` gap are both listed as drift with their nearest installed equivalent and nearest scale step. The reply names `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) as the owner of the decision, and no rounded value appears in code. A pass that cannot run is marked `NOT RUN` with a reason and the Figma check verdict is `INCOMPLETE`.\n+- Desired user-visible outcome: the agent reports each drift value with its nearest installed equivalent and the five-pass evidence, and explains that the design decision is handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) rather than made in code.\n+- Pass/fail: PASS if every unmatched value is reported as drift, the nearest token and scale step are named, and the decision is handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`). The verdict is `PASS` when all passes pass and `INCOMPLETE` when any pass is `NOT RUN` with a reason. FAIL if a value is rounded or written into code without being reported, or if `PASS` is reported despite a `NOT RUN` pass.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -48,13 +48,13 @@ PRE: Requires INSPECT-001 PASS (or MCP-001 PASS for the Framelink path), Figma D\n \n 1. `figma-ds-cli get` on the frame (read-only)  # -> raw values returned\n 2. `bash: rg -n '\"design-tokens\"' package.json`  # -> installed token package and version named\n-3. `bash: rg -n \"^4  8  12  16  24  32  48\" .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/SKILL.md`  # -> spacing scale the value must belong to\n+3. `bash: rg -n \"^4  8  12  16  24  32  48\" .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md`  # -> spacing scale the value must belong to\n 4. agent compares each value with the installed tokens, then with the scales  # -> unmatched values listed as drift\n-5. agent hands each drift value to `sk-design-fundamentals` and records the five-pass evidence  # -> nearest token and scale step named, nothing rounded into code\n+5. agent hands each drift value to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) and records the five-pass evidence  # -> nearest token and scale step named, nothing rounded into code\n \n | Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Name / Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n-| F2C-002 | Off-token frame values are reported as drift | Confirm a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift, not rounded into code | `This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.` | 1. Read the frame and record W -> 2. `bash: rg -n '\"design-tokens\"' package.json` -> 3. `bash: rg -n \"^4  8  12  16  24  32  48\" .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/SKILL.md` -> 4. compare each value with tokens then the scales -> 5. hand each drift value to `sk-design-fundamentals` and record the five-pass evidence | W and the state matrix are recorded. Pass 3a records resolved values and Pass 3b records the source search. Pass 4 lists each Figma variable and its installed-token result. Pass 5 records range and access verdicts. The `18px` padding and `44px` gap are listed as drift with a nearest token and scale step. Any `NOT RUN` pass gives an `INCOMPLETE` verdict. | Read-only transcript, W and captures, state matrix, render and source checks, variable-to-token table, range and access results, and the drift list with nearest values | PASS if every unmatched value is reported as drift with a nearest token and scale step, and the decision is handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`. The verdict is `PASS` when all passes pass and `INCOMPLETE` when any pass is `NOT RUN` with a reason. FAIL if a value is rounded or written into code without being reported, or if `PASS` is reported despite a `NOT RUN` pass. | Confirm the drift list names each frame value, its nearest installed token and its nearest scale step. Check that no rounded value appears in code. Require `INCOMPLETE` if any pass is `NOT RUN`. |\n+| F2C-002 | Off-token frame values are reported as drift | Confirm a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift, not rounded into code | `This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.` | 1. Read the frame and record W -> 2. `bash: rg -n '\"design-tokens\"' package.json` -> 3. `bash: rg -n \"^4  8  12  16  24  32  48\" .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md` -> 4. compare each value with tokens then the scales -> 5. hand each drift value to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) and record the five-pass evidence | W and the state matrix are recorded. Pass 3a records resolved values and Pass 3b records the source search. Pass 4 lists each Figma variable and its installed-token result. Pass 5 records range and access verdicts. The `18px` padding and `44px` gap are listed as drift with a nearest token and scale step. Any `NOT RUN` pass gives an `INCOMPLETE` verdict. | Read-only transcript, W and captures, state matrix, render and source checks, variable-to-token table, range and access results, and the drift list with nearest values | PASS if every unmatched value is reported as drift with a nearest token and scale step, and the decision is handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`). The verdict is `PASS` when all passes pass and `INCOMPLETE` when any pass is `NOT RUN` with a reason. FAIL if a value is rounded or written into code without being reported, or if `PASS` is reported despite a `NOT RUN` pass. | Confirm the drift list names each frame value, its nearest installed token and its nearest scale step. Check that no rounded value appears in code. Require `INCOMPLETE` if any pass is `NOT RUN`. |\n \n ### Optional Supplemental Checks\n \n@@ -70,8 +70,8 @@ Read the Project Profile Slot precedence in `SKILL.md` and confirm the reply fol\n |---|---|\n | [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md) | Root directory page and scenario summary |\n | [token-drift.md](token-drift.md) | Canonical per-feature execution contract |\n-| [`figma-to-code.md`](../../../../sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md) | Repo path `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md`; a row with no token is a finding to report, and workflow step 4 hands an open value to `sk-design-fundamentals` |\n-| [`SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/SKILL.md) | The design scales and the precedence order the drift decision is handed to |\n+| [`figma-to-code.md`](../../../../sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md) | Repo path `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md`; a row with no token is a finding to report, and workflow step 4 hands an open value to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) |\n+| [`web-visual-values.md`](../../../../sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md) | The design scales and the precedence order the drift decision is handed to |\n | [inspect.md](../../feature-catalog/inspect/inspect.md) | Feature-catalog source for the read-only node surface the scenario starts from |\n \n ### Implementation And Test Anchors\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\nindex 1e3e86260c..3953720aaa 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\n@@ -418,20 +418,20 @@ Prompt: `\"Build the summary card from this Figma frame using the tokens our app\n ### F2C-002 | Off-Token Frame Values Are Reported As Drift\n \n #### Description\n-Verify a frame value that matches no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift, with its nearest installed token and nearest scale step, and the decision is handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`. Rounding the value or pasting it into code is a failure.\n+Verify a frame value that matches no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift, with its nearest installed token and nearest scale step, and the decision is handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`). Rounding the value or pasting it into code is a failure.\n \n #### Scenario Contract\n Prompt: `\"This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.\"`\n \n-- Objective: confirm an off-token frame value is reported as drift and handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`\n-- Expected execution process: read the node without changing it and record W; compare each value against the installed tokens first and the design scales second; report every unmatched value as drift; hand the decision to `sk-design-fundamentals`; record the five-pass evidence\n-- Expected signals: the `18px` padding and `44px` gap are listed as drift with their nearest token and scale step; the reply names `sk-design-fundamentals` as the owner of the decision; nothing is rounded into code; any pass that cannot run is `NOT RUN` with a reason and the verdict is `INCOMPLETE`\n-- Desired user-visible outcome: the agent reports each drift value with its nearest installed equivalent and explains that the value decision is handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`\n+- Objective: confirm an off-token frame value is reported as drift and handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`)\n+- Expected execution process: read the node without changing it and record W; compare each value against the installed tokens first and the design scales second; report every unmatched value as drift; hand the decision to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`); record the five-pass evidence\n+- Expected signals: the `18px` padding and `44px` gap are listed as drift with their nearest token and scale step; the reply names `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) as the owner of the decision; nothing is rounded into code; any pass that cannot run is `NOT RUN` with a reason and the verdict is `INCOMPLETE`\n+- Desired user-visible outcome: the agent reports each drift value with its nearest installed equivalent and explains that the value decision is handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`)\n \n #### Test Execution\n | Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Name / Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n-| F2C-002 | Off-token frame values are reported as drift | Confirm a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift, not rounded into code | `This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.` | 1. Read the frame and record W -> 2. `bash: rg -n '\"design-tokens\"' package.json` -> 3. `bash: rg -n \"^4  8  12  16  24  32  48\" .skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/SKILL.md` -> 4. compare each value with tokens then the scales -> 5. hand each drift value to `sk-design-fundamentals` and record the five-pass evidence | W and the state matrix are recorded. Pass 3a records resolved values and Pass 3b records the source search. Pass 4 lists each Figma variable and its installed-token result. Pass 5 records range and access verdicts. The `18px` padding and `44px` gap are listed as drift with a nearest token and scale step. Any `NOT RUN` pass gives an `INCOMPLETE` verdict. | Read-only transcript, W and captures, state matrix, render and source checks, variable-to-token table, range and access results, and the drift list with nearest values | PASS if every unmatched value is reported as drift with a nearest token and scale step, and the decision is handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`. The verdict is `PASS` when all passes pass and `INCOMPLETE` when any pass is `NOT RUN` with a reason. FAIL if a value is rounded or written into code without being reported, or if `PASS` is reported despite a `NOT RUN` pass. | Confirm the drift list names each frame value, its nearest installed token and its nearest scale step. Check that no rounded value appears in code. Require `INCOMPLETE` if any pass is `NOT RUN`. |\n+| F2C-002 | Off-token frame values are reported as drift | Confirm a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift, not rounded into code | `This frame uses 18px padding and a 44px gap, which match nothing in our tokens. Implement it in the app.` | 1. Read the frame and record W -> 2. `bash: rg -n '\"design-tokens\"' package.json` -> 3. `bash: rg -n \"^4  8  12  16  24  32  48\" .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md` -> 4. compare each value with tokens then the scales -> 5. hand each drift value to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) and record the five-pass evidence | W and the state matrix are recorded. Pass 3a records resolved values and Pass 3b records the source search. Pass 4 lists each Figma variable and its installed-token result. Pass 5 records range and access verdicts. The `18px` padding and `44px` gap are listed as drift with a nearest token and scale step. Any `NOT RUN` pass gives an `INCOMPLETE` verdict. | Read-only transcript, W and captures, state matrix, render and source checks, variable-to-token table, range and access results, and the drift list with nearest values | PASS if every unmatched value is reported as drift with a nearest token and scale step, and the decision is handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`). The verdict is `PASS` when all passes pass and `INCOMPLETE` when any pass is `NOT RUN` with a reason. FAIL if a value is rounded or written into code without being reported, or if `PASS` is reported despite a `NOT RUN` pass. | Confirm the drift list names each frame value, its nearest installed token and its nearest scale step. Check that no rounded value appears in code. Require `INCOMPLETE` if any pass is `NOT RUN`. |\n \n > **Feature File:** [figma-to-code/token-drift.md](figma-to-code/token-drift.md)\n > **Catalog:** [inspect/inspect.md](../feature-catalog/inspect/inspect.md)\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex 39b0a9c0c5..82a48b2472 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -830,7 +830,7 @@ For details, see the [Skill Advisor README](.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor\n \n - Loads surface-aware patterns, checklists and verification recipes per surface, and detects the active stack from paths and library markers\n - Unsupported stacks (Go, React/Next.js server code, generic Node.js, React Native, Swift) trigger a quick disambiguation question\n-- Three surfaces. The native Skilled surface covers this framework's own code: scripts, MCP servers, agents, commands and skills. The CI surface holds GitHub Actions workflow standards for triggers, filters, permissions, timeouts, concurrency and caching, with scaffold templates. The Frontend surface holds stack-agnostic web frontend practice (markup, CSS, accessibility, i18n, performance, and the five-pass Figma check). A declared specific surface, including an operator-private surface skill, ranks ahead of Skilled\n+- Three surfaces. The native Skilled surface covers this framework's own code: scripts, MCP servers, agents, commands and skills. The CI surface holds GitHub Actions workflow standards for triggers, filters, permissions, timeouts, concurrency and caching, with scaffold templates. The Frontend surface holds stack-agnostic web frontend practice (markup, CSS, accessibility, i18n, performance, and the five-pass Figma check) plus the visual references that decide a value: spacing, type, colour and elevation scales, the hierarchy method and visual review pass, and motion and interaction rules. A declared specific surface, including an operator-private surface skill, ranks ahead of Skilled\n - Verifies before it claims done: three mandatory phases run implementation, then testing and debugging, then verification\n - A quality runner, `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, gives one verdict for a change in any repository. A committed `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` runs its commands only after `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true`. Without it each configured check reads `SKIPPED_UNTRUSTED`\n - A fixed hard floor and rules your repository can change. Secrets, conflict markers, parse failures, input validation and a few more stay on everywhere. Any other rule can be dropped or replaced in a `## Rule overrides` section of a checklist your repository declares, and `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off named comment-hygiene rules. A quality run applies those sections and records each accepted row, and a refused section stops it. This framework's own house style lives in the Skilled surface, so another repository does not inherit it\n@@ -899,16 +899,15 @@ One advisor identity routing to eight modes through `mode-registry.json`: five w\n - **`mcp-obsidian`:** manage Obsidian notes through `notesmd-cli`, the official `obsidian` CLI or an Obsidian MCP server\n - **`mcp-aside-devtools`:** drive the Aside AI browser through its CLI or its MCP\n - **`mcp-notion`:** work in a Notion workspace through the official Notion MCP, with direct API calls where the MCP falls short\n-- **`mcp-figma` _(transport)_:** read, author and export Figma designs through Figma Desktop and the `figma-ds-cli`. Reads and exports run freely, while authoring and destructive commands are gated. A read that feeds code ends at the five-pass check in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md`, and when the frame leaves a visual value open, or a value sits off every token and scale, that value goes to `sk-design-fundamentals`\n+- **`mcp-figma` _(transport)_:** read, author and export Figma designs through Figma Desktop and the `figma-ds-cli`. Reads and exports run freely, while authoring and destructive commands are gated. A read that feeds code ends at the five-pass check in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md`, and when the frame leaves a visual value open, or a value sits off every token and scale, that value goes to `sk-code-frontend`'s `web-visual-values.md`\n - **`mcp-refero` and `mcp-mobbin` _(transports)_:** read-only search of UI references and app screens and flows\n \n &nbsp;\n #### DESIGN\n \n-**`sk-design`** - parent hub for design work over four modes\n+**`sk-design`** - parent hub for design work over three modes\n \n - The hub carries no procedure of its own. It decides which mode owns the question in front of you and hands over\n-- **`sk-design-fundamentals`**, the default and the only mode without a command. Designs, builds and reviews any laid-out surface from fixed value scales: spacing, type, colour, contrast and hierarchy. Covers screen UI, slide decks, printed pages and document layouts, and adds interaction guidelines, motion principles and a WCAG review pass where the surface is a screen. Decides a visual value that `sk-code` or `mcp-figma` leaves open, or one that sits off every token and scale, in a fixed order: the repository's mode, the tokens the app installs, the design scales, then an optional `references/<project>-profile.md` that loads only when a request names that project's UI. Motion honours `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` on web and the platform setting on native, and nothing auto-plays\n - **`sk-design-md-generator`** behind `/design:extract`. Crawls a live URL across five viewports and writes a Style Reference `DESIGN.md` of named tokens, type scale, components and surfaces. Every value is copied from the running page and checked by script\n - **`sk-design-chart`** behind `/design:chart`. Turns the comparison a reader needs into one of 29 chart forms and ships it as a standalone HTML file with no build step and no remote dependency\n - **`sk-design-diagram`** behind `/design:diagram`. Self-contained HTML/SVG diagrams across 27 types with a skinnable editorial design system, plus ASCII and Markdown flowcharts and draw.io or Mermaid redraws\n@@ -989,9 +988,9 @@ One advisor identity routing to eight modes through `mode-registry.json`: five w\n - Picks the best `sk-prompt` framework, applies DEPTH at the right energy and validates with CLEAR\n - Returns a structured package (`FRAMEWORK`, `CLEAR_SCORE`, `RATIONALE`, `ENHANCED_PROMPT`, `ESCALATION_NOTES`). Used by the CLI mirror-card pipeline and by `/prompt:improve --agent` (`/prompt-improve` on Codex, Cursor, Pi and Hermes) when inline prompting is too weak\n \n-**Design** - owns design decisions and artifacts across four modes\n+**Design** - owns design decisions and artifacts\n \n-- Decides values and behavior through `sk-design-fundamentals`\n+- Decides values and behavior through the visual references in `sk-code-frontend`\n - Measures an existing surface into a Style Reference through `sk-design-md-generator`\n - Authors charts and diagrams through `sk-design-chart` and `sk-design-diagram`\n \n@@ -1530,7 +1529,7 @@ This repo ships as a **public template**. Of the skills it ships with, only one\n \n **`sk-design`** - ✅ codebase-agnostic\n \n-- Parent hub for design work over four modes: values and review (`sk-design-fundamentals`), design-reference extraction from a live URL into a v3 Style Reference `DESIGN.md` (`sk-design-md-generator`), standalone HTML charts across 29 forms (`sk-design-chart`), and HTML/SVG diagrams across 27 types (`sk-design-diagram`)\n+- Parent hub for design work over three modes: design-reference extraction from a live URL into a v3 Style Reference `DESIGN.md` (`sk-design-md-generator`), standalone HTML charts across 29 forms (`sk-design-chart`), and HTML/SVG diagrams across 27 types (`sk-design-diagram`)\n - Pairs with `sk-code` for the build. Works for any project\n \n **`system-spec-kit`** - ✅ codebase-agnostic\n@@ -1575,7 +1574,7 @@ This repo ships as a **public template**. Of the skills it ships with, only one\n &nbsp;\n #### What Adapting `sk-code` Looks Like\n \n-`sk-code` ships three surfaces. The Skilled surface holds the standards for this framework's own code, so keep it. The CI surface holds the GitHub Actions workflow standards. The Frontend surface holds stack-agnostic web frontend practice (markup, CSS, accessibility, i18n, performance, and the five-pass Figma check). Add a surface of your own when you want the hub to carry another stack.\n+`sk-code` ships three surfaces. The Skilled surface holds the standards for this framework's own code, so keep it. The CI surface holds the GitHub Actions workflow standards. The Frontend surface holds stack-agnostic web frontend practice (markup, CSS, accessibility, i18n, performance, and the five-pass Figma check) plus the visual references that decide a value: spacing, type, colour and elevation scales, the hierarchy method and visual review pass, and motion and interaction rules. Add a surface of your own when you want the hub to carry another stack.\n \n - **Remove a surface you do not use** by following [Remove A Surface](.skilled/skills/sk-code/README.md#remove-a-surface) in the sk-code README\n - **Add your own surface** with `/create:skill-parent update --surfaces <name>`, which scaffolds and registers it ahead of the Skilled surface\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md\nindex 7da8b2bdda..a3f6c799d1 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/SKILL.md\n@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@ name: mcp-figma\n description: \"Figma CLI orchestrator: drives figma-ds-cli for terminal Figma design work, with an optional Figma MCP via Code Mode.\"\n compatibility: Requires Figma Desktop (open with a file), Node.js >=18, and the silships figma-ds-cli on PATH. macOS is the supported baseline; the optional Figma MCP uses this project's Code Mode.\n allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, mcp__code_mode__call_tool_chain]\n-version: 1.2.1.0\n+version: 1.2.2.0\n user-invocable: true\n ---\n \n@@ -229,7 +229,7 @@ Scripts: `scripts/install.sh` (install + verify), `scripts/doctor.sh` (report-on\n \n Examples: [`examples/README.md`](examples/README.md) - safe worked walkthroughs mirroring the playbook scenarios (safe connect + daemon health, read-only inspect/export, optional MCP context pull). They live outside the `references/`/`assets/` discovery roots, so they are linked here rather than auto-loaded by the router.\n \n-Related skills: `sk-design-md-generator` (a measured Style Reference of extracted design tokens, applied whenever a read/export feeds a decision), `sk-code` (adapting exports into an app), `mcp-code-mode` (the optional MCP transport), `mcp-chrome-devtools` (browser preview only), and `system-spec-kit` when packet documentation or memory continuity applies. When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-design-fundamentals` and decide it there.\n+Related skills: `sk-design-md-generator` (a measured Style Reference of extracted design tokens, applied whenever a read/export feeds a decision), `sk-code` (adapting exports into an app), `mcp-code-mode` (the optional MCP transport), `mcp-chrome-devtools` (browser preview only), and `system-spec-kit` when packet documentation or memory continuity applies. When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) and decide it there.\n \n A Figma read that feeds code ends at the five-pass check in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md`.\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md\nindex 5ec937cf72..5d83d0e061 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/check-built-screen.md\n@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@ PRE: Requires INSPECT-001 PASS (or MCP-001 PASS for the Framelink path), Figma D\n \n ### Optional Supplemental Checks\n \n-Repeat Pass 5 contrast reads against the background each control actually sits on, not the page background, and compare them with the target `sk-design-fundamentals` sets.\n+Repeat Pass 5 contrast reads against the background each control actually sits on, not the page background, and compare them with the target `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`) sets.\n \n ---\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md\nindex a83eefe756..45c3aeb45b 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/mcp-tooling/mcp-figma/manual-testing-playbook/figma-to-code/token-drift.md\n@@ -12,7 +12,7 @@ This document captures the realistic user-testing contract, current behavior, ex\n \n ## 1. OVERVIEW\n \n-This scenario validates token drift reporting for `F2C-002`. It focuses on confirming that a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift and handed to `sk-design-fundamentals`, never rounded silently into code.\n+This scenario validates token drift reporting for `F2C-002`. It focuses on confirming that a frame value with no installed token and no scale step is reported as drift and handed to `sk-code-frontend` (`web-visual-values.md`), never rounded silently into code.\n \n ### Why This 