{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793873146765,"sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","part":{"partID":"prt_1ab2bf14f001N0OYz0OWNgqQ72","sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","messageID":"msg_1ab2bd540001MwmB7ajqKv7FMU","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b193b867a3efdabc","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && git show 10474a9567:.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/assets/token-starter-set.md | head -80"},"output":"---\ntitle: Token Starter Set\ndescription: What tokens.css contains, how to retune it for a project, and the role-layer rule that makes its dark mode work.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"starter design tokens css\"\n  - \"retune the token file\"\n  - \"semantic role layer tokens\"\n  - \"which grey for which text\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.1.0\n---\n\n# Token Starter Set\n\n`tokens.css` is a complete, contrast-verified expression of every scale in this skill, ready to copy into a project and retune.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nA project that starts from decided values instead of ad hoc ones, with components pointing at roles rather than raw ramps.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Starting a project with no established design system.\n- Adding a token layer to a codebase that currently hardcodes values.\n- Needing a worked example of a ramp that already satisfies the saturation and contrast rules.\n\n### Usage\n\nCopy `tokens.css` into the project's styles, then follow Section 5 to retune the hues. Components reference the semantic roles, never the raw ramps.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nCopy it in and retune the hues. Re-deriving the scales from prose wastes the work already done and reintroduces the ad hoc values the system exists to eliminate.\n\n### What It Is Not\n\nIt is not a theme, a component library, or a brand. It is the decisions, made once, with the hues chosen to be replaced.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHAT IS IN THE FILE\n\n| Group | Tokens | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spacing and sizing | `--space-1` through `--space-16` | 4px to 768px, no two neighbours closer than about 25% |\n| Type scale | `--text-xs` through `--text-7xl` | 12px to 72px, `px` values |\n| Weight | `--weight-normal`, `--weight-bold` | 400 and 600; nothing below 400 exists |\n| Line-height and measure | `--leading-tight` to `--leading-loose`, `--measure` | `--measure: 34em` keeps lines at 45 to 75 characters |\n| Greys | `--grey-100` through `--grey-900` | Cool-tinted, hue about 209 to 212. Each carries its measured contrast ratio in a comment |\n| Primary | `--primary-100` through `--primary-900` | Nine shades; `500` is the button-background shade |\n| Accents | `red`, `yellow`, `green` at `100`, `500`, `800` | The trio that \"flip the contrast\" needs: tint, solid fill, text on the tint |\n| Elevation | `--shadow-1` through `--shadow-5` | The five-elevation scale, with heavier alphas in dark mode |\n| Radius | `--radius`, `--radius-full` | 4px neutral default |\n| Border width | `--border-width`, `--border-width-thick` | 1px and 2px, for the \"keep the soft color, go to 2px\" fix |\n| Opacity | `--opacity-1` through `--opacity-6` | `.05` to `.8`, so overlays stop being eyeballed |\n| Semantic roles | `--surface`, `--text-primary`, `--action`, and the rest | The layer components actually reference |\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE ROLE LAYER\n\n**Reference the roles in components, not the raw ramps.** This is what makes the dark-mode block work: the dark override redefines only the roles, so every component that points at `--text-secondary` follows automatically, while a component pointing at `--grey-700` does not.\n\n```css\n/* Right - follows the mode */\n.card { background: var(--surface-raised); color: var(--text-primary); }\n\n/* Wrong - locked to light mode */\n.card { background: #fff; color: var(--grey-900); }\n```\n\nTwo roles are deliberately distinct and are the pair most often collapsed by mistake:\n\n- `--border` is **decorative** — dividers and separators, about 1.3:1. It has no contrast obligation.\n- `--border-strong` is **functional** — input outlines, checkbox edges, focus rings. Anything that is the only thing identifying a control needs 3:1 against its background under WCAG 1.4.11.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ntitle: Token Starter Set\ndescription: What tokens.css contains, how to retune it for a project, and the role-layer rule that makes its dark mode work.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"starter design tokens css\"\n  - \"retune the token file\"\n  - \"semantic role layer tokens\"\n  - \"which grey for which text\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.1.0\n---\n\n# Token Starter Set\n\n`tokens.css` is a complete, contrast-verified expression of every scale in this skill, ready to copy into a project and retune.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nA project that starts from decided values instead of ad hoc ones, with components pointing at roles rather than raw ramps.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Starting a project with no established design system.\n- Adding a token layer to a codebase that currently hardcodes values.\n- Needing a worked example of a ramp that already satisfies the saturation and contrast rules.\n\n### Usage\n\nCopy `tokens.css` into the project's styles, then follow Section 5 to retune the hues. Components reference the semantic roles, never the raw ramps.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nCopy it in and retune the hues. Re-deriving the scales from prose wastes the work already done and reintroduces the ad hoc values the system exists to eliminate.\n\n### What It Is Not\n\nIt is not a theme, a component library, or a brand. It is the decisions, made once, with the hues chosen to be replaced.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHAT IS IN THE FILE\n\n| Group | Tokens | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spacing and sizing | `--space-1` through `--space-16` | 4px to 768px, no two neighbours closer than about 25% |\n| Type scale | `--text-xs` through `--text-7xl` | 12px to 72px, `px` values |\n| Weight | `--weight-normal`, `--weight-bold` | 400 and 600; nothing below 400 exists |\n| Line-height and measure | `--leading-tight` to `--leading-loose`, `--measure` | `--measure: 34em` keeps lines at 45 to 75 characters |\n| Greys | `--grey-100` through `--grey-900` | Cool-tinted, hue about 209 to 212. Each carries its measured contrast ratio in a comment |\n| Primary | `--primary-100` through `--primary-900` | Nine shades; `500` is the button-background shade |\n| Accents | `red`, `yellow`, `green` at `100`, `500`, `800` | The trio that \"flip the contrast\" needs: tint, solid fill, text on the tint |\n| Elevation | `--shadow-1` through `--shadow-5` | The five-elevation scale, with heavier alphas in dark mode |\n| Radius | `--radius`, `--radius-full` | 4px neutral default |\n| Border width | `--border-width`, `--border-width-thick` | 1px and 2px, for the \"keep the soft color, go to 2px\" fix |\n| Opacity | `--opacity-1` through `--opacity-6` | `.05` to `.8`, so overlays stop being eyeballed |\n| Semantic roles | `--surface`, `--text-primary`, `--action`, and the rest | The layer components actually reference |\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE ROLE LAYER\n\n**Reference the roles in components, not the raw ramps.** This is what makes the dark-mode block work: the dark override redefines only the roles, so every component that points at `--text-secondary` follows automatically, while a component pointing at `--grey-700` does not.\n\n```css\n/* Right - follows the mode */\n.card { background: var(--surface-raised); color: var(--text-primary); }\n\n/* Wrong - locked to light mode */\n.card { background: #fff; color: var(--grey-900); }\n```\n\nTwo roles are deliberately distinct and are the pair most often collapsed by mistake:\n\n- `--border` is **decorative** — dividers and separators, about 1.3:1. It has no contrast obligation.\n- `--border-strong` is **functional** — input outlines, checkbox edges, focus rings. Anything that is the only thing identifying a control needs 3:1 against its background under WCAG 1.4.11.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && git show 10474a9567:.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/assets/token-starter-set.md | head -80\"}"},"time":{"start":1793873146191,"end":1793873146765}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793873146950,"sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","part":{"partID":"prt_1ab2bf1f2001zjYIFBh3GcRfd5","sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","messageID":"msg_1ab2bd540001MwmB7ajqKv7FMU","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-baeb9c20c9d1750d","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && for f in build-procedure diagnosis-table review-checklist color-system depth-and-detail hierarchy interaction-craft motion-principles ux-laws; do echo \"=== $f headings ===\"; git show 10474a9567:.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/references/$f.md 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"^#{2,3} \" | head -12; done"},"output":"=== build-procedure headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### When Not to Use\n## 2. THE SEVEN STEPS\n### 1. Start with a feature, not a layout\n### 2. Detail comes later\n### 3. Do not over-invest in low fidelity\n### 4. Design the smallest useful version, then build it\n### 5. Choose by elimination\n### 6. Start with too much white space and remove it\n=== diagnosis-table headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### How to Read the Tables\n## 2. HIERARCHY AND SPACING\n## 3. COLOR, CONTRAST AND BORDERS\n## 4. LAYOUT AND BREAKPOINTS\n## 5. TYPOGRAPHY DETAIL\n## 6. IMAGES AND COMPONENT SHAPE\n## 7. AFTER THE DIAGNOSIS\n=== review-checklist headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### How It Differs From Diagnosis\n### Source\n## 2. ACCESSIBILITY, CRITICAL\n## 3. ACCESSIBILITY, SERIOUS\n## 4. ACCESSIBILITY, MODERATE\n## 5. VISUAL AND COMPONENT CHECKS\n### Layout and spacing\n### Typography\n=== color-system headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Prerequisites\n## 2. WHY HSL\n## 3. BUILDING THE RAMP\n### Picking the base (500)\n### Finding the edges (900 and 100)\n### Filling the gaps\n## 4. KEEPING SATURATION ALIVE\n## 5. PERCEIVED BRIGHTNESS AND HUE ROTATION\n=== depth-and-detail headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Prerequisites\n## 2. EMULATING A LIGHT SOURCE\n## 3. TWO-PART SHADOWS\n### Three parallel systems, pick one\n### Shadow color, and the pure-black question\n### Animating a shadow\n### Full button anatomy\n## 4. DEPTH WITHOUT SHADOWS\n=== hierarchy headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### The Three Tiers\n## 2. CARRY EMPHASIS WITH WEIGHT AND COLOR\n## 3. EMPHASIZE BY DE-EMPHASIZING\n## 4. ACTIONS\n## 5. LABELS ARE A LAST RESORT\n## 6. BALANCE WEIGHT AGAINST CONTRAST\n## 7. VISUAL HIERARCHY IS NOT DOCUMENT HIERARCHY\n## 8. REFERENCES AND RELATED RESOURCES\n=== interaction-craft headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Source\n## 2. INPUTS AND FORMS\n## 3. POINTERS, TOUCH AND HIT AREAS\n## 4. TYPOGRAPHY IMPLEMENTATION\n### The `clamp()` tension\n## 5. FOCUS, KEYBOARD AND SCREEN READERS\n## 6. MOTION AT THE IMPLEMENTATION LEVEL\n## 7. PERFORMANCE\n=== motion-principles headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Sources\n### The Two Halves\n## 2. THE TWELVE PRINCIPLES, PART ONE\n### 1. Squash and stretch\n### 2. Anticipation\n### 3. Staging\n### 4. Straight ahead action and pose to pose\n### 5. Follow through and overlapping action\n=== ux-laws headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Boundary With The Sibling Skill\n## 2. INPUT AND TARGETS\n### Fitts's Law\n### Serial Position Effect\n## 3. CHOICE AND COMPLEXITY\n### Hick's Law\n### Miller's Law\n### Progressive Disclosure\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== build-procedure headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### When Not to Use\n## 2. THE SEVEN STEPS\n### 1. Start with a feature, not a layout\n### 2. Detail comes later\n### 3. Do not over-invest in low fidelity\n### 4. Design the smallest useful version, then build it\n### 5. Choose by elimination\n### 6. Start with too much white space and remove it\n=== diagnosis-table headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### How to Read the Tables\n## 2. HIERARCHY AND SPACING\n## 3. COLOR, CONTRAST AND BORDERS\n## 4. LAYOUT AND BREAKPOINTS\n## 5. TYPOGRAPHY DETAIL\n## 6. IMAGES AND COMPONENT SHAPE\n## 7. AFTER THE DIAGNOSIS\n=== review-checklist headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### How It Differs From Diagnosis\n### Source\n## 2. ACCESSIBILITY, CRITICAL\n## 3. ACCESSIBILITY, SERIOUS\n## 4. ACCESSIBILITY, MODERATE\n## 5. VISUAL AND COMPONENT CHECKS\n### Layout and spacing\n### Typography\n=== color-system headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Prerequisites\n## 2. WHY HSL\n## 3. BUILDING THE RAMP\n### Picking the base (500)\n### Finding the edges (900 and 100)\n### Filling the gaps\n## 4. KEEPING SATURATION ALIVE\n## 5. PERCEIVED BRIGHTNESS AND HUE ROTATION\n=== depth-and-detail headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Prerequisites\n## 2. EMULATING A LIGHT SOURCE\n## 3. TWO-PART SHADOWS\n### Three parallel systems, pick one\n### Shadow color, and the pure-black question\n### Animating a shadow\n### Full button anatomy\n## 4. DEPTH WITHOUT SHADOWS\n=== hierarchy headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### The Three Tiers\n## 2. CARRY EMPHASIS WITH WEIGHT AND COLOR\n## 3. EMPHASIZE BY DE-EMPHASIZING\n## 4. ACTIONS\n## 5. LABELS ARE A LAST RESORT\n## 6. BALANCE WEIGHT AGAINST CONTRAST\n## 7. VISUAL HIERARCHY IS NOT DOCUMENT HIERARCHY\n## 8. REFERENCES AND RELATED RESOURCES\n=== interaction-craft headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Source\n## 2. INPUTS AND FORMS\n## 3. POINTERS, TOUCH AND HIT AREAS\n## 4. TYPOGRAPHY IMPLEMENTATION\n### The `clamp()` tension\n## 5. FOCUS, KEYBOARD AND SCREEN READERS\n## 6. MOTION AT THE IMPLEMENTATION LEVEL\n## 7. PERFORMANCE\n=== motion-principles headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Sources\n### The Two Halves\n## 2. THE TWELVE PRINCIPLES, PART ONE\n### 1. Squash and stretch\n### 2. Anticipation\n### 3. Staging\n### 4. Straight ahead action and pose to pose\n### 5. Follow through and overlapping action\n=== ux-laws headings ===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n### Purpose\n### When to Use\n### Core Principle\n### Boundary With The Sibling Skill\n## 2. INPUT AND TARGETS\n### Fitts's Law\n### Serial Position Effect\n## 3. CHOICE AND COMPLEXITY\n### Hick's Law\n### Miller's Law\n### Progressive Disclosure\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals && for f in build-procedure diagnosis-table review-checklist color-system depth-and-detail hierarchy interaction-craft motion-principles ux-laws; do echo \\\"=== $f headings ===\\\"; git show 10474a9567:.skilled/skills/sk-design/sk-design-fundamentals/references/$f.md 2>/dev/null | grep -E \\\"^#{2,3} \\\" | head -12; done\"}"},"time":{"start":1793873146354,"end":1793873146950}}}}
