{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793873662422,"sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","part":{"partID":"prt_1ab33b1f60021YG1gna7QcfRJE","sessionID":"ses_e54d7f46fffe0ZLISbmnuV6zfy","messageID":"msg_1ab33a6f8001SR1OuEVU6ME22b","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-83975e2cbb95401e","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Deep Review Iteration 5: Cross-Cutting Close-Out\n\n## Dimension\n\nMaintainability and traceability close-out. Scope: re-check the active P1, confirm each earlier\nfinding still stands, and audit the last two reach surfaces the operator named: the visual-review\nload set and the hub canary that is supposed to protect the new vocabulary.\n\n## Review Focus Addressed\n\nOperator focus, final pass over all five named concerns: correctness, lost guidance, dangling\nreferences to fundamentals, routing reach and doc accuracy. This iteration replays the failing\nroute, maps the visual-review load set against the checks it contains, and confirms that the new\nvocabulary has regression coverage in the hub canary.\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/ROUTER.md:105-130`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md:474-590`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/hub-router.json:90`\n- `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/001-sk-code/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json:180-210`\n- `specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/deep-review-findings-registry.json`\n- `specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/steer.md` (absent)\n\nCommand evidence used:\n\n- `compiled-route --hub sk-code` for \"design review\", \"review this design\", \"make this ui look\n  designed\", \"visual hierarchy is flat\" and \"the page looks amateur\"\n- `grep -n 'WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY' -A6 ROUTER.md` (three-file load set)\n- `grep -n 'web-accessibility-patterns.md#a' web-visual-hierarchy.md` (seven A-anchor links)\n- `python3` count of the sk-code canary cases and their expected modes (9 cases; the frontend case\n  is `surface-bundle-frontend` with the pre-existing CASCADE prompt)\n- a read of the reducer registry after iteration 4 (7 open findings, 0 resolved)\n\n## Findings by Severity\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone. The adversarial replay re-ran the failing route for R1-P1-001; it fails the same way, and\nit is a routing-reach defect, not a blocker with destructive or security impact.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone new. R1-P1-001 remains active: \"design review\" and \"review this design\" still resolve to\n`sk-code-review` alone, while \"make this ui look designed\" and \"visual hierarchy is flat\" reach\n`sk-code-frontend`. The boundary is the review-class keyword, not the visual vocabulary as a whole.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n8. **The visual-review intent does not load the accessibility reference it cites** -- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/ROUTER.md:112` -- `WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY` resolves `frontend-practice.md`, `web-visual-hierarchy.md` and `web-visual-values.md`. The loaded review pass at `web-visual-hierarchy.md:474-590` sends the reviewer to `web-accessibility-patterns.md` for A2 (accessible name), A3 (roving tabindex), A5 (focus-visible), A8 (forced colors), A13 (menu button), A14 (tooltip) and A17 (error summary), and the ROUTER's own rule says a row loads what it resolves and nothing more.\n   Case: an agent follows the documented load set for a visual-review request and reports a focus\n   finding without A8's forced-colors rule, or an icon-button finding without A2's name order.\n   The checks are named in the table, but the criterion each finding must cite lives in a file the\n   intent did not load.\n   Finding class: class-of-bug\n   Scope proof: the three-file map is the only stage-two row for this intent; the seven A-anchors\n   are the only external references in the review pass, and `web-accessibility-patterns.md` is not\n   in the map. The A11Y intent loads the file, so the split is intentional there, not accidental.\n   Affected surface hints: [\"sk-code-frontend ROUTER\", \"web-visual-hierarchy review pass\", \"web-accessibility-patterns\"]\n   Recommendation: add `references/web-accessibility-patterns.md` to the `WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY`\n   load set, or state in the review pass that the A-anchors are follow-on reads outside the intent.\n\n9. **The hub canary never exercises the new visual vocabulary** -- `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/001-sk-code/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json:190` -- The sk-code canary has nine cases. Its only frontend bundle case is `surface-bundle-frontend` with the pre-existing prompt \"review my css cascade layers change\". None of the 75 new `code-frontend-visual` phrases appears in the fixture, so the regression harness cannot catch the failure in finding 1 or a future tie-break that drops the new class.\n   Case: a later edit removes \"what padding should this have\" from `code-frontend-visual` or\n   reorders the tie-break. The canary suite still passes, and the only failing check is the\n   operator-facing playbook scenario, which covers one intent and is not the hub's route gold.\n   Finding class: class-of-bug\n   Scope proof: parsed all nine canary cases; none of their prompts contains a `code-frontend-visual`\n   keyword, and the new class has no case id. The surface-hint unit test covers a different phrase\n   family (\"css cascade layers\").\n   Affected surface hints: [\"sk-code canary\", \"compiled-route regression\", \"code-frontend-visual\"]\n   Recommendation: add one canary per new intent (values, hierarchy, motion), including the\n   \"design review\" phrase that currently fails.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n| Protocol | Level | Status | Evidence |\n|----------|-------|--------|----------|\n| `spec_code` | core | partial | All structural claims hold except the routing handoff; R1-P1-001 is active. |\n| `checklist_evidence` | core | partial | All rows are `Met`; two recording defects remain (R2-P2-003, R2-P2-004). |\n| `skill_agent` | overlay | pass | The five agent mirrors name the new references; no capability grant changed. |\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | overlay | pass | Canonical and mirrors agree on the decide path. |\n| `feature_catalog_code` | overlay | notApplicable | No feature-catalog entry changed. |\n| `playbook_capability` | overlay | pass | The visual-values scenario replays; its 5/5 suite result stands. |\n\n## Adversarial P0/P1 Replay\n\n- R1-P1-001: re-ran `compiled-route --hub sk-code --prompt \"design review\"` and\n  `--prompt \"review this design\"`. Both return `route single [sk-code-review]`. Confirmed active.\n  The alternative explanation (the review workflow loads the frontend guidance itself) is still\n  unsupported: `sk-code-review`'s packet is a review-cache workflow, and the surface is only\n  bundled when the hub decision names it.\n- No P0 candidate exists, so no Hunter/Skeptic/Referee promotion is needed this iteration.\n- No finding was downgraded or marked false positive.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- The `WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY` accessibility gap is not a lost rule: the checks are inline and the\n  linked file exists. It is a load-set mismatch, so the finding stays P2.\n- The canary gap is a coverage finding, not a production failure. It becomes material only when\n  combined with a routing regression, which is exactly what finding 1 shows.\n- One prompt, \"review this screen\", still bundles the frontend surface correctly. The failure is\n  specific to phrases that contain the review-class signal and no other bundle key.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- The registry after iteration 4 holds 7 open findings, 0 resolved, with the expected severity\n  split P0=0, P1=1, P2=6.\n- Every iteration file 1-4 is present and its final line is one of the three legal verdicts.\n- The new references' item ranges are complete; the moved token file has no external dependency.\n- `steer.md` was absent at every read, so no lead ruling changed this lineage's direction.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- **A second active P1 hiding in the load-set or canary findings.** Ruled out: both are coverage\n  mismatches with no prompt that produces a wrong route beyond R1-P1-001.\n- **A stale chart citation.** Ruled out in iterations 2 and 3; the remaining \"research\" hits are\n  records.\n- **A fundamentals path in any live doc.** Ruled out in iteration 1; the only hits are records and\n  generated retrieval data.\n\n## Next Focus\n\nNone. The stop policy is `max-iterations` and iteration 5 of 5 is complete. Synthesis compiles the\nreport with the active registry: P0=0, P1=1, P2=8, verdict CONDITIONAL, stopReason\n`maxIterationsReached`.\n\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\n","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals/specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Deep Review Iteration 5: Cross-Cutting Close-Out\\n\\n## Dimension\\n\\nMaintainability and traceability close-out. Scope: re-check the active P1, confirm each earlier\\nfinding still stands, and audit the last two reach surfaces the operator named: the visual-review\\nload set and the hub canary that is supposed to protect the new vocabulary.\\n\\n## Review Focus Addressed\\n\\nOperator focus, final pass over all five named concerns: correctness, lost guidance, dangling\\nreferences to fundamentals, routing reach and doc accuracy. This iteration replays the failing\\nroute, maps the visual-review load set against the checks it contains, and confirms that the new\\nvocabulary has regression coverage in the hub canary.\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/ROUTER.md:105-130`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md:474-590`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/hub-router.json:90`\\n- `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/001-sk-code/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json:180-210`\\n- `specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/deep-review-findings-registry.json`\\n- `specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/steer.md` (absent)\\n\\nCommand evidence used:\\n\\n- `compiled-route --hub sk-code` for \\\"design review\\\", \\\"review this design\\\", \\\"make this ui look\\n  designed\\\", \\\"visual hierarchy is flat\\\" and \\\"the page looks amateur\\\"\\n- `grep -n 'WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY' -A6 ROUTER.md` (three-file load set)\\n- `grep -n 'web-accessibility-patterns.md#a' web-visual-hierarchy.md` (seven A-anchor links)\\n- `python3` count of the sk-code canary cases and their expected modes (9 cases; the frontend case\\n  is `surface-bundle-frontend` with the pre-existing CASCADE prompt)\\n- a read of the reducer registry after iteration 4 (7 open findings, 0 resolved)\\n\\n## Findings by Severity\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone. The adversarial replay re-ran the failing route for R1-P1-001; it fails the same way, and\\nit is a routing-reach defect, not a blocker with destructive or security impact.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone new. R1-P1-001 remains active: \\\"design review\\\" and \\\"review this design\\\" still resolve to\\n`sk-code-review` alone, while \\\"make this ui look designed\\\" and \\\"visual hierarchy is flat\\\" reach\\n`sk-code-frontend`. The boundary is the review-class keyword, not the visual vocabulary as a whole.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n8. **The visual-review intent does not load the accessibility reference it cites** -- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/ROUTER.md:112` -- `WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY` resolves `frontend-practice.md`, `web-visual-hierarchy.md` and `web-visual-values.md`. The loaded review pass at `web-visual-hierarchy.md:474-590` sends the reviewer to `web-accessibility-patterns.md` for A2 (accessible name), A3 (roving tabindex), A5 (focus-visible), A8 (forced colors), A13 (menu button), A14 (tooltip) and A17 (error summary), and the ROUTER's own rule says a row loads what it resolves and nothing more.\\n   Case: an agent follows the documented load set for a visual-review request and reports a focus\\n   finding without A8's forced-colors rule, or an icon-button finding without A2's name order.\\n   The checks are named in the table, but the criterion each finding must cite lives in a file the\\n   intent did not load.\\n   Finding class: class-of-bug\\n   Scope proof: the three-file map is the only stage-two row for this intent; the seven A-anchors\\n   are the only external references in the review pass, and `web-accessibility-patterns.md` is not\\n   in the map. The A11Y intent loads the file, so the split is intentional there, not accidental.\\n   Affected surface hints: [\\\"sk-code-frontend ROUTER\\\", \\\"web-visual-hierarchy review pass\\\", \\\"web-accessibility-patterns\\\"]\\n   Recommendation: add `references/web-accessibility-patterns.md` to the `WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY`\\n   load set, or state in the review pass that the A-anchors are follow-on reads outside the intent.\\n\\n9. **The hub canary never exercises the new visual vocabulary** -- `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/001-sk-code/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json:190` -- The sk-code canary has nine cases. Its only frontend bundle case is `surface-bundle-frontend` with the pre-existing prompt \\\"review my css cascade layers change\\\". None of the 75 new `code-frontend-visual` phrases appears in the fixture, so the regression harness cannot catch the failure in finding 1 or a future tie-break that drops the new class.\\n   Case: a later edit removes \\\"what padding should this have\\\" from `code-frontend-visual` or\\n   reorders the tie-break. The canary suite still passes, and the only failing check is the\\n   operator-facing playbook scenario, which covers one intent and is not the hub's route gold.\\n   Finding class: class-of-bug\\n   Scope proof: parsed all nine canary cases; none of their prompts contains a `code-frontend-visual`\\n   keyword, and the new class has no case id. The surface-hint unit test covers a different phrase\\n   family (\\\"css cascade layers\\\").\\n   Affected surface hints: [\\\"sk-code canary\\\", \\\"compiled-route regression\\\", \\\"code-frontend-visual\\\"]\\n   Recommendation: add one canary per new intent (values, hierarchy, motion), including the\\n   \\\"design review\\\" phrase that currently fails.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n| Protocol | Level | Status | Evidence |\\n|----------|-------|--------|----------|\\n| `spec_code` | core | partial | All structural claims hold except the routing handoff; R1-P1-001 is active. |\\n| `checklist_evidence` | core | partial | All rows are `Met`; two recording defects remain (R2-P2-003, R2-P2-004). |\\n| `skill_agent` | overlay | pass | The five agent mirrors name the new references; no capability grant changed. |\\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | overlay | pass | Canonical and mirrors agree on the decide path. |\\n| `feature_catalog_code` | overlay | notApplicable | No feature-catalog entry changed. |\\n| `playbook_capability` | overlay | pass | The visual-values scenario replays; its 5/5 suite result stands. |\\n\\n## Adversarial P0/P1 Replay\\n\\n- R1-P1-001: re-ran `compiled-route --hub sk-code --prompt \\\"design review\\\"` and\\n  `--prompt \\\"review this design\\\"`. Both return `route single [sk-code-review]`. Confirmed active.\\n  The alternative explanation (the review workflow loads the frontend guidance itself) is still\\n  unsupported: `sk-code-review`'s packet is a review-cache workflow, and the surface is only\\n  bundled when the hub decision names it.\\n- No P0 candidate exists, so no Hunter/Skeptic/Referee promotion is needed this iteration.\\n- No finding was downgraded or marked false positive.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- The `WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY` accessibility gap is not a lost rule: the checks are inline and the\\n  linked file exists. It is a load-set mismatch, so the finding stays P2.\\n- The canary gap is a coverage finding, not a production failure. It becomes material only when\\n  combined with a routing regression, which is exactly what finding 1 shows.\\n- One prompt, \\\"review this screen\\\", still bundles the frontend surface correctly. The failure is\\n  specific to phrases that contain the review-class signal and no other bundle key.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- The registry after iteration 4 holds 7 open findings, 0 resolved, with the expected severity\\n  split P0=0, P1=1, P2=6.\\n- Every iteration file 1-4 is present and its final line is one of the three legal verdicts.\\n- The new references' item ranges are complete; the moved token file has no external dependency.\\n- `steer.md` was absent at every read, so no lead ruling changed this lineage's direction.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- **A second active P1 hiding in the load-set or canary findings.** Ruled out: both are coverage\\n  mismatches with no prompt that produces a wrong route beyond R1-P1-001.\\n- **A stale chart citation.** Ruled out in iterations 2 and 3; the remaining \\\"research\\\" hits are\\n  records.\\n- **A fundamentals path in any live doc.** Ruled out in iteration 1; the only hits are records and\\n  generated retrieval data.\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\nNone. The stop policy is `max-iterations` and iteration 5 of 5 is complete. Synthesis compiles the\\nreport with the active registry: P0=0, P1=1, P2=8, verdict CONDITIONAL, stopReason\\n`maxIterationsReached`.\\n\\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\\n\", \"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/316-deprecate-design-fundamentals/specs/sk-design/021-deprecate-fundamentals/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793873654262,"end":1793873662422}}}}
